'''Jahmaal Noel Fyffe'''<ref>{{cite news|last=Collins|first=Hattie Collins|title=The grime star Chipmunk: Diamond Rings|url=http://entertainment.timesonline.co.uk/tol/arts_and_entertainment/music/article6716272.ece|work=[[The Sunday Times]]|publisher=[[News International|Times Newspapers Ltd]]|access-date=13 February 2011|date=19 July 2009|location=London}}{{dead link|date=September 2024|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> (born 26 November 1990),<ref name="dob">{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/music/artists/54266e9c-7098-48ea-bb70-e25fdfa14227#p00b0bzjis|title=Artists - Chipmunk|publisher=The BBC|access-date=13 November 2010|archive-date=8 November 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141108023226/http://www.bbc.co.uk/music/artists/54266e9c-7098-48ea-bb70-e25fdfa14227#p00b0bzjis|url-status=dead}}</ref> better known by his stage name '''Chip''' (formerly '''Chipmunk'''), is an English rapper and grime MC. In the past 14 years, he has collaborated with the likes of [[Skepta]], [[T.I.]], [[Meek Mill]], [[D-Block Europe|Young Adz]] and many others. In 2009, he released his debut album, ''[[I Am Chipmunk]]'', featuring four songs which peaked in the top 10 of the [[UK Singles Chart]], including the chart-topping "[[Oopsy Daisy]]". In 2011, Chipmunk released his follow-up album, the American hip-hop-influenced ''[[Transition (Chipmunk album)|Transition]]''. It included the single "[[Champion (Chipmunk song)|Champion]]" featuring [[Chris Brown]], which peaked at number two on the UK Singles Chart.

Chip moved to Atlanta in 2012 and signed to T.I.'s [[Grand Hustle Records|Grand Hustle]] label. On Christmas Day, Chip released "London Boy", featuring the likes of Meek Mill, Skepta, Movado, plus more.

Following his return to London, Chip embarked on a year-long rap beef which solidified his slogan "Chip Can't Run Out of Bars". Following this, he has since released six projects, with his most recent being ''Grime Scene Saviour'' featuring the likes of JME, Scorcher, Skepta, Frisco and D Double E among others.

==Early life==
Jahmaal Noel Fyffe was born on 26 November 1990 in Tottenham, and is the son of Jamaican immigrant parents.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news|url=http://www.thefader.com/2016/12/08/chip-interview-power-up-konnichiwa-mobo-grime|title=Inside Chipmunk's Decade-Long Journey To Become One Of Grime's Most Honest Voices|work=The Fader|access-date=17 September 2017|language=en}}</ref> Fyffe's parents later separated. Fyffe received the nickname "Chipmunk" from a friend (who was later stabbed to death) on account of him being "short and chubby with big teeth".<ref name=":1">{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2011/apr/10/chipmunk-interview-transition-every-gyal|title=Chipmunk: 'I want to be called a genius'|last=Manzoor|first=Sarfraz|date=9 April 2011|work=The Observer|access-date=17 September 2017|language=en-GB|issn=0029-7712}}</ref> Fyffe began rapping after hearing [[Wiley (musician)|Wiley]] and [[Dizzee Rascal]] on pirate radio when he was a child,<ref name=":0" /> later inspiring him to rent studios out for £20 to record music with his friends when he was just thirteen years old.<ref name=":1" /> Jahmaal attended [[Highgate Wood Secondary School]] in [[Crouch End]], before transferring to [[Gladesmore Community School]], citing that there were problems for him at Highgate Wood.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/education/2009/aug/26/chipmunk-a-levels|title=Why Chipmunk is chirpy about his A-levels|last=Kintue|first=Vivien|date=26 Aug 2009|website=The Guardian|access-date=2019-06-02}}</ref> After completing secondary school education, he underwent his A-Levels at [[Haringey Sixth Form Centre]], achieving a B in Drama, C in Sociology and D in Critical Thinking.<ref name=":2" />

Chip has been an [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] fan since childhood. Prior to a music career, Chip originally aspired to be a footballer.<ref>{{cite web|title=SB.TV - Chipmunk @ Arsenal Emirates Stadium|website=YouTube|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=w-IvrtDekBA  |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211215/w-IvrtDekBA |archive-date=2021-12-15 |url-status=live|date=3 April 2015 |access-date=19 December 2015}}{{cbignore}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/people/profiles/my-secret-life-chipmunk-rapper-18-1799791.html|title=My Secret Life: Chipmunk, rapper, 18|website=Independent.co.uk|date=9 October 2009 }}</ref>

==Career==
Like most [[grime music|grime]] MCs, Fyffe joined [[musical collective|musical crews]] and utilised [[pirate radio]] to promote his sound to a wider audience during his breakthrough. He began MCing aged fourteen, and in his early career he associated with Double S's [[Northumberland Park, London|Northumberland Park]]-based group Shoddy Crew.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.eventim.co.uk/artist/chipmunk/|title=Chipmunk Tickets - Eventim|work=[[Eventim UK]]|accessdate=2026-05-09}}</ref><ref name="vice"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mixtapemadness.com/blog/music/the-rise-of-chip|title=The Rise of Chip|work=[[Mixtape Madness]]|author=Frimpong, Fiona|date=2011-09-16}}</ref> He first gained mainstream recognition{{citation needed|date=February 2024}} when [[Wiley (musician)|Wiley]] brought him to [[Tim Westwood]]'s radio show in 2007 along with Ice Kid and they performed a freestyle.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qt9tfVyxggU |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211215/qt9tfVyxggU |archive-date=2021-12-15 |url-status=live|title=Wiley, Chipmunk & Ice Kid freestyle - Westwood|date=12 July 2007 |access-date=22 October 2021|publisher=[[YouTube]]}}{{cbignore}}</ref> By 2008, he had his own crew, Circular Movements, alongside fellow MCs Shalo and Hits.<ref name="1x">{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/1xtra/hotfor2008/chipmunk.shtml|title=Hot for 2008: Chipmunk|work=[[BBC Radio 1Xtra]]|date=2008}}</ref>

Fyffe signed a record deal with [[Jive Records]] of [[Sony Music|Sony Music UK]].<ref name="sony">{{cite web|url=http://www.sonymusic.co.uk/artists/chipmunk|title=Chipmunk|publisher=[[Sony Music]]|access-date=13 November 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101107013022/http://www.sonymusic.co.uk/artists/chipmunk/|archive-date=7 November 2010|url-status=dead}}</ref> He has successfully released eleven top-ten hits on the [[UK Singles Chart]], of which "[[Oopsy Daisy]]" debuted at #1. He released his debut album, ''[[I Am Chipmunk]]'' in 2009, which charted at #2 on the [[UK Albums Chart]]. His second album, ''[[Transition (Chipmunk album)|Transition]]'' was released on 18 April 2011.

On 13 January 2012, he announced via [[Twitter]] that he had permanently abbreviated his name from "Chipmunk" to "Chip". Fyffe announced his signing to American rapper [[T.I.]]'s [[Grand Hustle Records]] in March 2012 after leaving Sony following the closing of Jive.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rap-up.com/2012/03/01/ti-signs-iggy-azalea-chipmunk-and-trae-tha-truth-to-grand-hustle/|title=Rap-Up.com -- T.I. Signs Iggy Azalea, Chipmunk, and Trae Tha Truth to Grand Hustle|work=Rap-Up.com}}</ref>

At the start of 2015, Chipmunk announced his two-part ''Believe & Achieve'' EP project – with ''EPisode 1'' to be released independently on Monday 4 May via his own label Cash Motto. The EP features productions from New Machine ([[Plan B (musician)|Plan B]]) and Chris Loco ([[Labrinth]]) as well as collaborations with Irish troubadour [[Maverick Sabre]] and [[Grime (music genre)|grime]] legends [[D Double E]], [[Jammer (rapper)|Jammer]], [[Stormzy]], [[Kano (rapper)|Kano]], [[Wretch 32]] and Shalo.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://bluereignmg.com/chip-reveals-details-for-new-ep-believe-achieve-episode-1/ |title=Chip Reveals Details for New EP Believe & Achieve: Episode 1 |publisher=Blue Reign Media GRP Limited |date=20 February 2015 |access-date=23 April 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150518085809/http://bluereignmg.com/chip-reveals-details-for-new-ep-believe-achieve-episode-1/ |archive-date=18 May 2015 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Chipmunk runs a company called Cash Motto, formerly Cash Motivation, which he uses to release his music and clothing.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.cashmottoclothing.com/about-us |title=About Us |publisher=Cash Motto Clothing |access-date=7 July 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://twitter.com/OfficialChip/status/657685766613704706 |title=Chipmunk On Twitter: "It's My Company. Clothing Is The Clothing. But All Under The Brolly Lad. " |publisher=[[Twitter]] |access-date=7 July 2016}}</ref> The company has also released numerous singles by Chipmunk's friend and frequent collaborator Shalo. In 2016, he won Best Grime Act at the MOBO Awards 2016.

===2006–2010: ''I Am Chipmunk'' and UK breakthrough===
In 2006, at the age of sixteen, he uploaded the song "Who Are You?" which received a lot of underground success{{citation needed|date=February 2024}} and has reached over 3,000,000 views on YouTube. At the 2008 Mobo Awards, Chipmunk won the Best Newcomer Award while being signed to an independent label, Alwayz Recordings. Soon after, in late-2008, just before his eighteenth birthday, Chipmunk signed a high-figure record deal{{citation needed|date=February 2024}} with [[Columbia Records]], [[Sony Music]] and began recording his debut album. On 2 March 2009, he released his first single from the album, "[[Chip Diddy Chip]]". The single charted at #21 on the [[UK Singles Charts]] and was written by himself.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.officialcharts.com/search/singles/My%20Man |title=Chipmunk - Chip Diddy Chip |publisher=[[Official Charts Company]] |access-date=15 May 2011}}</ref> The music video for the track featured [[Tim Westwood]] and [[Dappy]] of [[N-Dubz]], and was filmed at his old secondary school, [[Gladesmore Community School]].<ref>[[Chip Diddy Chip|Chip Diddy Chip music video was filmed in his secondary school]]</ref> After the release of the single, he announced his album would be titled, ''[[I Am Chipmunk]]''. Following the release of "[[Diamond Rings (song)|Diamond Rings]]" which featured guest vocals from [[Emeli Sandé]]. The song became Sandé's and Chipmunk's first top 10 hit, after it charted at #6 on the UK Singles Charts. This made Chipmunk a pioneer in doing so, as he became the youngest UK rapper to ever score a top 10 charted song.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.officialcharts.com/search/singles/Feels%20Like%20Home |title=Chipmunk Ft Emeli Sande - Diamond Rings |publisher=[[Official Charts Company]] |access-date=15 May 2011}}</ref>

In October 2009, Chipmunk released his biggest track to date "Oopsy Daisy" as the third official single. It featured guest vocals from singer [[Dyo (singer)|Dayo Olatunji]] and was written by [[Talay Riley]]. "Oopsy Daisy" is Chipmunk's most successful single to date. The track debuted at #1 on the [[UK Singles Charts]] and later gained a certification of Gold, by the [[British Phonographic Industry|BPI]]. Chipmunk became the youngest rapper to have scored a UK #1 single.<ref name="myspace.com">{{cite web|url=https://www.myspace.com/djironik/photos/35743352 |title=New Nation Single Review - Sep 2008 Photos from Ironik (Ironik) on Myspace |publisher=Myspace.com |date=21 April 2011 |access-date=15 May 2011}}</ref> The track was Chipmunk's first single released internationally. The song was accompanied by a music video, Chipmunk's love interest was played by Red Madrell, a British actress.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.songfacts.com/detail.php?id=17148 |title=Oopsy Daisy by Chipmunk Songfacts |publisher=Songfacts.com |date=11 October 2009 |access-date=15 May 2011}}</ref> The song beat British [[girl-group]], [[The Saturdays]] to number-one with their come-back single, "[[Forever Is Over]]", and spent over a month in the top 10.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.digitalspy.co.uk/music/news/a181559/chipmunk-beats-the-saturdays-to-no1.html |title=Chipmunk beats The Saturdays to No.1 - Music News |website=Digital Spy |date=11 October 2009 |access-date=15 May 2011}}</ref> After the release of ''I Am Chipmunk'' on 12 October 2009, it gained mainly positive reviews from critics. It debuted at #2 on the [[UK Albums Chart]].<ref>{{cite news| url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/8313270.stm | work=BBC News | title=Burke beats Williams to top chart | date=18 October 2009}}</ref> However, the album charted at #1 on the [[UK R&B Chart|UK R&B Albums Chart]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/radio1/chart/rnbalbums |title=Radio 1 - Chart - UK Top 40 RNB Albums |publisher=BBC |date=8 May 2011 |access-date=15 May 2011 |archive-date=23 July 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100723181421/http://www.bbc.co.uk/radio1/chart/rnbalbums |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Chipmunk announced that he was going to release a platinum edition of the album with new tracks after I am Chipmunk sold over 300,000 copies. The first single released from ''I Am Chipmunk'' was "[[Until You Were Gone (Chipmunk song)|Until You Were Gone]]" which featured guest vocals from [[Esmée Denters]]. The single charted at #3 on the UK Singles Charts. ''[[I Am Chipmunk|I Am Chipmunk: The Platinum Edition]]'' was released on 3 May 2010. It contained four new songs.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.amazon.co.uk/dp/B003D3NFD0 |title=I Am Chipmunk - Platinum Edition: Chipmunk: Amazon.co.uk: Music |publisher=Amazon.co.uk |access-date=15 May 2011}}</ref> The album then gained a Platinum Certification by the BPI. After making a gross income of £1,550,000 the previous year, making him the highest-paid UK rapper. Chipmunk also performed at the Mobo Awards in 2009, Capital Summertime Ball in 2010 at [[Wembley Stadium]] and at the Brit Awards.<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/features/chipmunk--a-level-headed-player-1939528.html | location=London | work=The Independent | title=Chipmunk - A level headed player | date=9 April 2010}}</ref>

===2010–11: ''Transition'' and "Champion" featuring Chris Brown===
Chipmunk announced he was working on a new studio album in late-2010, and confirmed it would be titled, ''[[Transition (Chipmunk album)|Transition]]'' which he stated it would be different from the first album, stating: "It kinda represents the growth in me as a person since my last album, so the sound has matured as well as I have and that’s the main thing for me with the record - I just wanted the sound, the sound and the feel of the album to feel… I feel like a granddad but you know, just more mature than my first album, you know life’s changed." The album features American artists [[Keri Hilson]], [[Chris Brown]], [[Trey Songz]]<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.chipdaily.net/2011/02/chipmunk-transition-artwork-tracklist.html |title=Chipmunk 'Transition' (Artwork + Tracklist) |publisher= ChipDaily.net|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120727111138/http://www.chipdaily.net/2011/02/chipmunk-transition-artwork-tracklist.html|archive-date=27 July 2012|url-status=dead|access-date=6 March 2016}}</ref> and [[Kalenna Harper]] from [[Diddy-Dirty Money]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.soulculture.co.uk/blogs/music-blog/album-tracklists/chipmunk-transition-album-cover-x-tracklisting/ |title=Chipmunk - Transition &#124; Album Cover x Tracklisting |website=SoulCulture.co.uk |access-date=7 July 2016}}</ref> and British rapper Wretch 32.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.taletela.com/news/4947/chipmunk-talks-chris-brown-keri-hilson-and-transition |title=Chipmunk Talks Chris Brown, Keri Hilson and 'Transition'... - TaleTela |access-date=16 March 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110320023026/http://www.taletela.com/news/4947/chipmunk-talks-chris-brown-keri-hilson-and-transition |archive-date=20 March 2011 }}</ref> He released a [[promotional single]] onto [[iTunes]] on 12 November 2010. The single charted at #72 on the UK Singles Charts,<ref>{{cite web |title=Chip {{!}} full Official Chart History {{!}} Official Charts Company |url=https://www.officialcharts.com/artist/5422/chip/ |website=www.officialcharts.com}}</ref> with limited promotion.<ref>{{cite web |title=Flying High by Chip - Songfacts |url=https://www.songfacts.com/facts/chipmunk/flying-high |website=www.songfacts.com |access-date=24 February 2021 |language=en}}</ref>
In early-2011, he confirmed that "[[Champion (Chipmunk song)|Champion]]" would be released as the first mainstream single from the album, and the single would feature Chris Brown. The single was written by Harmony "H-Money" Samuels, Jahmaal Fyffe, Eric Bellinger, Erika Nuri.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.metro.co.uk/music/855186-chipmunk-champion-is-already-no-1-in-my-heart |title=Chipmunk: 'Champion is already No 1 in my heart' |publisher=Metro.co.uk |date=10 February 2011 |access-date=15 May 2011}}</ref> The single charted at #2 on the UK Singles Charts, being held off the top spot by [[Jessie J]] and [[B.o.B]] with their entry with "[[Price Tag]]". However, both songs sold over 90,000 copies in their first week and Jessie J gained the top spot by 5%.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.theofficialcharts.com/chart-news/jessie-j-wins-champion-chart-battle-as-lady-gaga-swoops-into-top-3/ |title=Jessie J wins Champion chart battle as Lady Gaga swoops into Top 3 |publisher=Theofficialcharts.com |date=13 February 2011 |access-date=15 May 2011}}</ref> The song serves as the theme for the reality competition show [[WWE Tough Enough]]. It was announced that "In the Air" would be released as the second mainstream single released from ''[[Transition (Chipmunk album)|Transition]]'' the single would feature guest vocals from American R&B singer, [[Keri Hilson]]. The single was released on 11 March 2011.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.amazon.co.uk/In-The-Air-Radio-Edit/dp/B004P9R8IO |title=In The Air (radio edit): Chipmunk Feat. Keri Hilson: Amazon.co.uk: MP3 Downloads |publisher=Amazon.co.uk |access-date=21 December 2011}}</ref> The album ''Transition'' itself attained its UK release through Sony Music's Jive label on 18 April 2011.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.bluesandsoul.com/feature/659/chipmunk_back_to_take_the_title/ |title=Chipmunk interview by Pete Lewis, 'Blues & Soul' April 2011 |publisher=Bluesandsoul.com |access-date=21 December 2011}}</ref> He makes a cameo appearance in Wretch 32 and [[Example (rapper)|Example]]'s video for [[Unorthodox (Wretch 32 song)|Unorthodox]].

The third single to be released from the album is titled "Take Off" and features Trey Songz.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.officialchipmunk.com/gb/news/2011-05-19/new_single_take_off_ft_trey_songz/ |title=New single- 'Take Off' ft Trey Songz — CHIPMUNK — News |publisher=Officialchipmunk.com |access-date=21 December 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110628131941/http://www.officialchipmunk.com/gb/news/2011-05-19/new_single_take_off_ft_trey_songz/ |archive-date=28 June 2011 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Chipmunk also released a net video for the song "Every Gyal" featuring Mavado.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VTrOiYe-2oQ&ob=av2e  |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211215/VTrOiYe-2oQ |archive-date=2021-12-15 |url-status=live|title=Chipmunk Feat. Mavado - Every Gyal |publisher=YouTube |date=26 April 2011 |access-date=21 December 2011}}{{cbignore}}</ref> In August 2011, Chipmunk performed a free concert in [[Dumfries]], [[Scotland]] and in [[Wales]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.xavia.co.uk |title=Xavia |publisher=Xavia |access-date=21 December 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111227024245/http://www.xavia.co.uk/ |archive-date=27 December 2011 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.youthbeatz.co.uk |title=Youth Beatz |publisher=Youth Beatz |access-date=21 December 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.liveeventsgroup.co.uk/news/team-chip-and-the-live-events-group |title=Team Chip and the Live Events Group &#124; Wedding DJ Hire &#124; Mobile Disco Hire |date=28 November 2011 |publisher=Live Events Group |access-date=7 July 2016}}</ref>

===2012–14: Grand Hustle and London Boy Mixtape===
On 7 October 2011, RCA Music Group announced it was disbanding [[Jive Records]], along with Arista Records and J Records. With the shutdown, Chip, and all other artists previously signed to these three labels, had the option to release their future material on the [[RCA Records]] brand. Chip chose not to do so, and certain media outlets speculated that he may be dropped from the label.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.pappzd.com/2011/09/chipmunk-dropped-by-record-label-sony/ |title=Is Chip Being Dropped From His Label? |publisher=Pappzd.com |date=28 September 2011 |access-date=21 December 2011 |url-status=usurped |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111222040524/http://pappzd.com/2011/09/chipmunk-dropped-by-record-label-sony/ |archive-date=22 December 2011  }}</ref> However, Chip instead decided to sign with [[Grand Hustle]], the label of fellow rapper and collaborator, [[T.I.]]<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.billboard.biz/bbbiz/industry/record-labels/rca-s-new-executive-team-named-under-ceo-1005324782.story |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110829031727/http://www.billboard.biz/bbbiz/industry/record-labels/rca-s-new-executive-team-named-under-ceo-1005324782.story |url-status=dead |archive-date=29 August 2011 |title=RCA's New Executive Team Named Under CEO Peter Edge Amid Layoffs (Update) |publisher=Billboard.biz |date=23 August 2011 |access-date=21 December 2011}}</ref> In December 2011, Chipmunk announced that he would be shortening his stage name to "Chip", under the advice and instruction of T.I., and that all of his future releases would appear under his new stage name.

In January 2012, Chip announced via his official Twitter account that he had been working with [[Sean Garrett]] and T.I. on his next project, but did not reveal the name of the project in question. He also posted a picture of himself recording in a studio in [[Atlanta]] with T.I. Shortly after the announcement, Chip revealed that throughout the year, he would be posting a series of online video blogs to keep his fans updated on what he was up to. On 10 February 2012, Chip uploaded his own freestyle version of fellow rapper [[Tyga]]'s smash hit, "[[Rack City]]" to his official [[YouTube]] account. The video received over 70,000 views and 10,000 likes within the first 48 hours of release.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PZCmEPj4-8Y |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120210224524/http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PZCmEPj4-8Y&gl=US&hl=en |archive-date=2012-02-10 |url-status=dead|title=Chip - Rack City (Freestyle)|work=YouTube}}</ref>

Chip returned to the studio to complete recording on the project during August–September 2012, before unveiling his single "Londoner", a collaboration with Wretch 32, [[Professor Green]] and [[Loick Essien]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yWRT0xhN5pw |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211215/yWRT0xhN5pw |archive-date=2021-12-15 |url-status=live|title=Chip - Londoner feat Wretch 32, Professor Green & Loick Essien (Official Video)|work=YouTube|date=25 November 2012 }}{{cbignore}}</ref> The track was released as the project's third underground single on 25 November 2012. On 9 October 2012, Chip was featured on the annual [[BET Hip Hop Awards]] cypher, alongside his Grand Hustle labelmates [[Iggy Azalea]], B.o.B, T.I. and [[Trae tha Truth]].<ref name="cypher">{{cite web|url=http://www.bet.com/video/hiphopawards/2012/cypher/the-grand-hustle-cypher.html |title=Hip Hop Awards Exclusives: The Grand Hustle Cypher |publisher=Black Entertainment Television LLC |work=[[Black Entertainment Television|BET]] |date=9 October 2012 |access-date=9 April 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130422040538/http://www.bet.com/video/hiphopawards/2012/cypher/the-grand-hustle-cypher.html |archive-date=22 April 2013  }}</ref> On 10 December, Chip revealed the track listing of the mixtape, including collaborations with labelmates Iggy Azalea, Trae Tha Truth, T.I, previous collaborators [[Skepta]], [[Mavado (singer)|Mavado]], Wretch 32 and Professor Green as well as [[Meek Mill]] and [[Young Jeezy]]. He also re-confirmed the release date of 25 December 2012, and revealed the tape would be available to download free of charge.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.grmdaily.com/news/whats-hot/chip-reveals-tracklisting-for-london-boy/attachment/image004/|title=image004|access-date=19 December 2012|archive-date=10 November 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131110125225/http://grmdaily.com/news/whats-hot/chip-reveals-tracklisting-for-london-boy/attachment/image004/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

===2014–present: ''Believe & Achieve'', Grime Beef & League of My Own II===
On 31 October 2014, Chip dropped his "Fright Night" freestyle on YouTube, and announced to fans that his new project ''Believe & Achieve'' would be coming in 2015.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://ukflowzone.com/?p=732 |title=Chip x Chiraq – Fright Night Freestyle |publisher=UKFlowZone |date=9 November 2014 |access-date=26 February 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402125238/http://ukflowzone.com/?p=732 |archive-date=2 April 2015 |url-status=dead }}</ref> In November 2014, Chip collaborated with hotly tipped [[Stormzy]] and Shalo on "I’m Fine" which he filmed on his twenty-fourth birthday and released shortly afterwards.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.mobo.com/news-blogs/new-music-chip-feat-stormzy-shalo-im-fine |title=New Music: Chip Feat. Stormzy & Shalo "I'm Fine" |publisher=MOBO |date=26 November 2014 |access-date=26 February 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180902234853/http://www.mobo.com/news-blogs/new-music-chip-feat-stormzy-shalo-im-fine |archive-date=2 September 2018 |url-status=dead }}</ref> A remix of "I’m Fine" featuring Frisco, [[Krept and Konan]] appeared online on 16 January 2015 via i-D Magazine.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://i-d.co/article/premiere-chip-ft-frisco-krept-konan-im-fine-remix/ |title=premiere: chip ft frisco, krept & konan, i'm fine (remix) |publisher=i-D |date=16 January 2015 |access-date=13 March 2015}}</ref> On 1 January 2015, Chip dropped the track ‘School of Grime’ via his [[SoundCloud]] page,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://soundcloud.com/officialchip/school-of-grime-chip-feat-d-double-e-jammer|title=Chip - School Of Grime Feat. D Double E \u0026 Jammer by Chip CM - Free Listening on SoundCloud|work=SoundCloud|access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref> and followed by a music video a couple weeks later. The track featured grime legends D Double E and Jammer and instantly started blowing up at specialist radio, online and across the urban world for weeks.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://rwdmag.com/exclusive-interview-chip-announces-new-e-p-release-and-asks-the-question-whats-more-important-the-music-or-where-its-coming-from |title=Exclusive Interview: Chip Announces New E.P. Releases and Asks the Question "What's More Important? The Music? Or Where It's Coming From?" |publisher=RWD Magazine |date=22 January 2015 |access-date=13 March 2015}}</ref> After the success of the original song "School of Grime" featuring D Double E and Jammer, Chip released a remix blended with UK Garage classic,"The Streets - Has It Come to This" and it was accompanied by a new music video which contained footage of a live performance at student event, Rampage. [6]<ref>{{cite AV media|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UhHEw-jfL0k|title=Chip ft. D Double E x Jammer - School Of Grime (The Streets Remix) [Music Video]: SBTV|date=5 March 2015|work=YouTube|access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref>

On Saturday 24 January, Chip appeared for the second time on Charlie Sloth's trademark show ''Fire In The Booth'' on 1Xtra, which had a huge listenership and was trending on Twitter.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/newsbeat-30973529 |title=Chipmunk criticises Tinie for not concentrating on MCing |work=BBC News |date=25 January 2015 |access-date=13 May 2015}}</ref> In Chip's freestyle, he opened up some questions about the UK rap scene and caused controversy by calling out [[Tinie Tempah]] for his current lack of commitment to music.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.digitalspy.co.uk/music/news/a624161/chip-criticises-tinie-tempah-he-was-rude-to-unsigned-rappers.html |title=Chip criticises Tinie Tempah: 'He was rude to unsigned rappers' |website=[[Digital Spy]] |date=26 January 2015 |access-date=13 May 2015}}</ref> The YouTube video from ''Fire In The Booth'' instantly went viral with 100k views in just a few days, and was followed by a 1Xtra debate which also trended on Twitter.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://twitter.com/dellessa/status/560877604681834497 |title=Dellessa on Twitter: "Yes - we're trending #1XtraDebate 🙌 Thanks for getting involved 😊👍👏" |publisher=[[Twitter]] |date=29 January 2015 |access-date=7 July 2016}}</ref> Chip also took part in ITV's new entertainment show ''Get Your Act Together'' on 25 January, having been invited by the Shaolin Monks to learn Kung Fu martial arts.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.itv.com/presscentre/ep2week5/get-your-act-together |title=Get Your Act Together |website=Itv.com|date=25 January 2015 |access-date=13 May 2015}}</ref> The performance culminated in him balancing on two spear points to widespread audience acclaim.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.primarytimes.net/west_london/news/2015/02/shaolin-monks-return-to-uk-for-first-time-in-seven-years |title=Shaolin Monks Return To UK For First Time In Seven Years |date=February 2015 |access-date=13 May 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924081728/http://www.primarytimes.net/west_london/news/2015/02/shaolin-monks-return-to-uk-for-first-time-in-seven-years |archive-date=24 September 2015 |url-status=dead }}</ref> He competed against three other celebrity teams and won the round. Chip performed with the Shaolin Monks in the final of the show on 22 February 2015<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.reveal.co.uk/lifestyle/news/a630331/sundays-tv-pick-get-your-act-together-grand-finale.html |title=Get Your Act Together Finale|website=Reveal.co.uk |date=22 February 2015 |access-date=13 May 2015}}</ref>

A remix freestyle of ‘School of Grime’ was organically conceived by Chip, D Double E and Jammer in the back of a cab after the official video shoot. Using [[The Streets]]' ‘Has It Come To This’ as the beat, a freestyle was filmed in one take by a camera phone was shot and then uploaded to YouTube. The track was then recorded properly in the studio and premiered via Pigeons and Planes on 21 February<ref>{{cite web|url=http://pigeonsandplanes.com/2015/02/chip-ft-d-double-e-and-jammer-school-of-grime-x-the-streets-remix |title=Premiere of School Of Grime x The Streets Freestyle |date=21 February 2015 |access-date=13 May 2015}}</ref> A week later, a visual for the remix was made by legendary Risky Roadz and hosted on SBTV channel.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UhHEw-jfL0k |title=School Of Grime x The Streets Music Vide|website=Youtube.como |date=5 March 2015}}</ref>

On 20 March 2015, Chip released a freestyle ‘Pepper Riddim’ hitting back at 5 Rappers who had dissed him since his ''Fire In The Booth.''<ref>{{cite web|url=http://grmdaily.com/video/chip-snaps-back-on-tinie-tempah-big-narstie-saskilla-dj-cameo-on-pepper-riddim |title=Chip Snaps Back on Tinie Tempah, Big Narstie, Bugzy Malone, Saskilla & DJ Cameo on "Pepper Riddim"|website=Grmdaily.com |date=20 March 2015 |access-date=13 May 2015}}</ref>
‘The End’ was then filmed in one take and released to put an end to the negativity coming from fellow artists in the grime scene, and to look forward to the next chapter of Chip with a positive outlook.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://grmdaily.com/video/chip-responds-to-all-the-controversy-with-the-end-music-video/ |title=Chip responds to all the controversy with the end music video|website=Grmdaily.com |date= 3 April 2015 |access-date=13 May 2015}}</ref>

On Christmas Day 2015, Chip released his tenth mixtape, ''[[Rap vs. Grime]]'', for free download. The project, which features a guest appearance from [[Stormzy]], consists of remixes of hit songs from 2015 such as "[[My Way (Fetty Wap song)|My Way]]" and "[[Classic Man]]" (the "rap" songs) and [[diss track]]s aimed at [[Tinie Tempah]] and [[Bugzy Malone]] (the "grime" songs). Also on Christmas Day 2015, Chip released "[[Hear Dis]]" featuring Stormzy.

Afters six years since the release of his last album, Chip released his third album; and his first independent album, ''[[League of My Own II]]''. This album charted at #12 in the UK album charts which is his third Top 20 album.

Chip's fourth studio album ''[[Ten10 (album)|Ten10]]'' was released on 21 September 2018 through his independent record label Cash Motto. It featured singles CRB Check ft Not3s, Right Now ft JME & Frisco & the lead single My Girl ft Red Rat.

In March 2020, Chip announced a surprise album with fellow rappers Skepta and Young Adz. ''[[Insomnia (Skepta, Chip and Young Adz album)|Insomnia]]'' debuted in at number 3 in the official UK charts with 2 top 40 singles.

Chip released a tribute for longtime friend and collaborator [[Black the Ripper]] who died on 4 April 2020. The single titled "[[420 (cannabis culture)|0420]]" was released on 20 April 2020 and sampled Black the Ripper's single "Missing You".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://grmdaily.com/chip-0420-rip-black-the-ripper/|title=Premiere: Chip Pays Tribute To The Late Black The Ripper On "0420"|website=Grmdaily.com|date=20 April 2020|access-date=22 October 2021}}</ref>

==Feuds==
===Tinie Tempah===
In 2015, Chip appeared on [[Charlie Sloth]]'s radio show where he called out fellow London based rapper [[Tinie Tempah]] over the latter's interests and perceived exploitation of their relationship for personal benefit.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.capitalxtra.com/artists/tinie-tempah/news/chip-started-beef-to-get-hype-around-new-ep/#LVJJUHPL061f345Z.97|title=Tinie Tempah Thinks Chip Started Beef To Get Hype Around His New EP|date=15 May 2015|work=Capital XTRA|access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.gigwise.com/news/97551/chip-slams-tinie-tempah-on-fire-in-the-booth|title=British rapper Chip fires shots at Tinie Tempah on Charlie Sloth's radio show|author=Peter Kandunias|work=gigwise.com|access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref> On 9 October 2015 he released "96 Bars of Revenge" in which he dissed both Tempah and [[Bugzy Malone]].<ref name="Genius Lyrics">{{cite web |title=Chip – 96 Bars of Revenge |url=https://genius.com/Chip-96-bars-of-revenge-lyrics |access-date=24 February 2021 |language=en}}</ref> He then followed this up with "Coward", which was aimed directly at Tempah.<ref>{{cite web |title=Tinie Tempah then mocked Chip about losing his record deal with Sony. |url=https://www.capitalxtra.com/artists/tinie-tempah/lists/chip-beef-history/preview-new-mixtape/ |website=Capital XTRA |language=en}}</ref>

===Bugzy Malone===
[[Bugzy Malone]] released his "Fire in the Booth", a freestyle session hosted by [[BBC 1Xtra]] presenter Charlie Sloth, on 14 March 2015, mentioning Chip.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ukgrime.co/2015/09/15/chip-vs-bugzy-again/ |title=Chip vs Bugzy Malone |access-date=5 November 2015 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151117034655/http://www.ukgrime.co/2015/09/15/chip-vs-bugzy-again/ |archive-date=17 November 2015 }}</ref> Chip replied to Malone's critics including him on his war dub "Pepper Riddim". This started the feud and drew attention to the grime scene early on in 2015. Malone released a war dub for Chip entitled "Relegation Riddim", travelling to Chip's hometown of [[Tottenham]] to film the video for the song.

Five months later, Chip released a number of tracks in his defence starting with "Light Work" on 11 September 2015. He then went on to release "Run Out Riddim" and "Hat Trick" in the same week. These three riddems was all uploaded on three different prominent UK platforms one after the other. GRMdaily, Link Up tv and SBTV. Around a month later on 9 October 2015 he released "96 Bars of Revenge" in which he dissed both Malone and Tinie Tempah. The rivalry continued as Malone then replied back with "Wasteman", Chip replied within 7 hours with "Dickhead", Malone then released the pre-recorded track "Zombie Riddim" via his Twitter account straight after "Dickhead" was uploaded. Malone stated that he would clash with Chip on [[Lord of the Mics]], a battle rap show hosted by [[Boy Better Know]] member Jammer, for the right amount of money, and Chip has also stated that he would be willing as long as he would be clashing against both Malone and Tempah.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://grmdaily.com/all-you-need-to-know-about-the-grime-beef-so-far/ |title=Updated: All you need to know about the Grime beef so far &#124; GRM Daily - Grime, Rap music and Culture |publisher=GRM Daily |date=5 April 2015 |access-date=7 July 2016}}</ref>

===Yungen===
This feud started when Chip mentioned [[Yungen]] in "96 Bars of Revenge". Yungen replied the following year on his track "Comfy", which sparked the feud further. On 19 February 2016, Chip released "One Take" (freestyle) on his YouTube channel, dissing Yungen.<ref>{{cite web|title = Chip Shells Shots On Shots On Shots In His 'One Take' Freestyle|url = http://www.mtv.co.uk/the-wrap-up/blog/chip-shells-shots-on-shots-on-shots-in-his-one-take-freestyle|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160225110325/http://www.mtv.co.uk/the-wrap-up/blog/chip-shells-shots-on-shots-on-shots-in-his-one-take-freestyle|url-status = dead|archive-date = 25 February 2016|website = Mtv.co.uk|access-date = 22 February 2016}}</ref> Yungen released a pre-recorded track one hour later titled "Punk (Shitmunk Diss)" which was a warning to Chip to take his freestyle down.<ref>{{cite web|title = Yungen Replies To Chip With Punk (Shitmunk Diss)|url = http://www.mtv.co.uk/tags/yungen-replies-to-chip-with-punk-shitmunk-diss|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160305003252/http://www.mtv.co.uk/tags/yungen-replies-to-chip-with-punk-shitmunk-diss|url-status = dead|archive-date = 5 March 2016|website = Mtv.co.uk|access-date = 22 February 2016}}</ref> Chip replied in 24 hours with "Michelle Riddim", a diss track towards Yungen and Tinie Tempah.<ref>{{cite web|title = Premiere: Chipmunk responds to Yungen with "Michelle Riddim (Lil Clive Diss)" {{!}} GRM Daily - Grime, Rap music and Culture|url = http://grmdaily.com/video/chipmunk-responds-yungen-diss-michelle-riddim/|website = GRM Daily - Grime, Rap music and Culture|date = 20 February 2016|access-date = 22 February 2016|language = en-US}}</ref> Yungen responded quickly with "Oopsy Daisy Riddim",<ref>{{cite web|title = Watch: Yungen lets loose on 'Oopsy Daisy Riddim' - SBTV|url = http://www.sbtv.co.uk/watch/music-videos/yungen-lets-loose-on-oopsy-daisy-riddim/|website = SBTV|access-date = 22 February 2016|language = en-US|last = SBTV}}{{Dead link|date=November 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> and Chip has since responded once again with "L (Lil' Clive Diss 2)".<ref>{{cite web|title = Chipmunk responds to Yungen's "Oopsy Daisy Riddim" with "L (Lil Clive Diss 2)" {{!}} GRM Daily - Grime, Rap music and Culture|url = http://grmdaily.com/video/premiere-chipmunk-responds-to-yungen-again/|website = GRM Daily - Grime, Rap music and Culture|date = 22 February 2016|access-date = 22 February 2016|language = en-US}}</ref>
Recently{{when|date=January 2020}} the 'Oopsy Daisy Riddim (Shitmunk Diss) was taken down from [[YouTube]] after it contained a sample of Chipmunk's single "Oopsy Daisy", it was then re-loaded, this time with the copyrighted audio removed. After Yungen was filmed running from a man who gripped the rapper's arms, dropping his [[Nando's]] meal in the process, he released the track "Away Games". In response to a couple of slights from the track, Chip released his track "Peri Peri Sauce", aimed at the South London rapper.<ref>{{Citation|last=Link Up TV {{!}} Music|title=Chip - Peri Peri Sauce [Music Video] @OfficialChip {{!}} Link Up TV|date=10 August 2016|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=i9kTL88TMbc|access-date=3 December 2017}}</ref>

===Stormzy===
Chip became embroiled in a feud with fellow musician Stormzy, releasing diss tracks 'Killer MC' and 'Flowers' in October 2020.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Moore |first1=Sam |title="Listen to Chip take shots at Stormzy on fiery new diss track 'Flowers'" |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/chip-takes-shots-at-stormzy-on-flowers-2776081 |website=NME |date=8 October 2020 }}</ref> This was in response to an incident in June, where Stormzy had entered his apartment block, argued with his family and refused to leave.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2020-10-07|title=Chip hits out at Stormzy for showing up at his house after feud|url=https://metro.co.uk/2020/10/07/chip-hits-out-at-stormzy-for-showing-up-at-his-house-after-feud-as-he-shares-video-13388464/|access-date=2020-11-15|website=Metro|language=en}}</ref> In January 2021, artist Ghetts released a track called "Skengman" featuring Stormzy where Stormzy referenced Chip on a number of lines. Soon after, Chip released a track called "10 Commandments", addressing Stormzy.  July 2021 fellow artist [[Dave (rapper)|Dave]] released a track called "Clash" featuring Stormzy where Stormzy briefly addressed the previous incident. Hours later Chip released an audio track entitled "Clash?" where he responded mainly to Stormzy but also referenced Dave in the response.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2021-07-16|title=Chip responds to diss by stromzy on a track by dave|url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/chip-responds-to-stormzys-subliminal-clash-diss-with-new-track-2990013/|access-date=2021-07-16|website=NME|language=en}}</ref>

